"""Property-specific planning worksheets; no performance or pricing promises."""
# slug, title, intro, steps (heading, explanation), example, worksheet fields
PLAYBOOKS = [
('furniture-damage-claim-workflow','Furniture damage claims: a rental receiving workflow',
 'Receiving furniture is an inspection task, not simply a signature at the door. Build a record that connects the order, carton, product and damage while the seller’s reporting window is still open.', [
 ('Before delivery','Save the order confirmation, model, finish, quantity and seller’s current damage-reporting process. Note deadlines and who can authorize a return. Give the receiver access to this information before the appointment; an owner who is unreachable during delivery cannot resolve every exception remotely.'),
 ('At the receiving location','Photograph the packaging, shipping label and any visible damage. Follow the carrier and seller procedures for noting exceptions. Open and inspect goods when the receiving agreement allows it, and distinguish carton damage from product damage. Avoid discarding evidence before the claim instructions are understood.'),
 ('Create a single claim record','Describe the defect precisely and attach the relevant photos, order line and delivery date. Request the available remedy and record the case number. Keep the item segregated from ready-to-install inventory so an installer does not accidentally use it or discard its packaging.'),
 ('Close the loop','Assign a person to follow up until the remedy arrives or the refund is confirmed. Reconcile replacement quantities and inspect replacement goods too. Update the installation plan when a missing item affects room readiness, and preserve a temporary solution that meets the property’s actual needs.')],
 'In a hypothetical delivery, one of six dining chairs has a split leg. The host records the exact chair, photographs the label and defect, and keeps it out of service. The installation tracker shows five usable chairs until a remedy is received; the order is not marked complete merely because six cartons arrived.',
 ['Vendor and order number','SKU, finish and quantity','Arrival date and reporting deadline','Packaging photos','Product defect and evidence','Requested remedy and case number','Responsible person and next follow-up','Replacement inspection and close date']),
('rental-furniture-substitution-approval','How to approve furniture substitutions without losing the plan',
 'An out-of-stock item should trigger a short fit and scope review. A visually similar replacement may have different dimensions, care requirements or delivery timing. Use an approval record before the substitute is ordered.', [
 ('Identify the role of the original item','Record why it was selected: dimensions, seating capacity, material, finish, cleaning method and delivery date. Distinguish non-negotiable requirements from preferences. A sofa chosen to pass a narrow stairwell cannot be replaced based on color alone.'),
 ('Compare the actual specification','Put the original and proposed item side by side. Check packed and assembled dimensions, weight, access, care instructions and parts availability. Confirm that the change does not create a second substitution elsewhere, such as a larger rug or a smaller coffee table.'),
 ('Approve the complete cost','List product price, freight, assembly, return implications and any installation delay. Name the person who approves changes and record approval before purchase. An informal message saying that something looks fine should not replace a record of what was actually accepted.'),
 ('Update downstream records','Replace the product in the order tracker, floor plan, care inventory and installation brief. Notify the person receiving goods so the new model is not treated as an unexplained mismatch. Keep the original decision visible for future reference.')],
 'A hypothetical replacement bedside table is cheaper but wider. On the floor plan it prevents a closet door from opening fully. The host rejects it despite the price saving and requests a narrower alternative with the same storage function.',
 ['Original item and reason selected','Proposed SKU and finish','Assembled and packed dimensions','Care and replacement differences','Product and logistics cost change','Effect on related items','Approver and approval date','Updated documents and recipients']),
('rental-design-measurement-handoff','The measurement handoff for a remote rental designer',
 'Remote design starts with trustworthy site information. A room’s width and length are only part of the brief: openings, obstructions and the delivery route determine whether the finished plan can be installed.', [
 ('Create a consistent room record','Give every room a unique name and use it on photos, videos and sketches. Record units and the person who measured. Include full wall lengths, ceiling features and fixed elements. Mark unclear readings for remeasurement instead of silently estimating.'),
 ('Measure openings and movement','Record door and window positions relative to a wall corner, opening direction and relevant trim. Include closets, radiators, outlets and built-in furniture. Photograph each wall straight on and add a walkthrough that shows how rooms connect.'),
 ('Trace the delivery route','Start at the unloading location and record stairs, turns, lifts and entrances. Distinguish clear opening dimensions from the decorative door size. Request carrier guidance for difficult routes; a room plan alone is not proof that a large item can be delivered.'),
 ('Confirm before irreversible purchases','Send the designer a checklist of what is measured and what remains uncertain. Require critical dimensions to be verified before ordering items with costly return or custom terms. Keep revisions dated so the installer does not use an outdated plan.')],
 'In a hypothetical townhouse, the living room easily fits a sofa, but the stair landing does not permit the frame to turn. A delivery-route sketch catches the issue before purchase and prompts evaluation of a smaller or sectional alternative.',
 ['Property and room identifier','Measured by, date and units','Wall lengths and ceiling features','Opening locations and swing','Fixed elements and obstructions','Photograph filenames','Delivery route constraints','Unverified dimensions and follow-up owner']),
('rental-design-installation-punch-list','A room-by-room punch list for rental furnishing installation',
 'Installation is complete when the property is ready for its intended use, not when the truck leaves. Keep a punch list that records unresolved fit, condition, assembly and inventory issues with a responsible person and a close date.', [
 ('Check against the approved plan','Walk each room with the furniture list and latest layout. Confirm models, colors and quantities, then inspect condition. Separate missing goods from assembly issues and design changes so each item reaches the person able to resolve it.'),
 ('Test the guest setup','Open drawers and doors, extend tables and unfold any convertible furniture as instructed. Check how guests reach controls, charging points and storage. Review the arrangement in daytime and evening lighting. Refer installation or safety concerns to the qualified person responsible rather than improvising a fix.'),
 ('Record evidence and ownership','Use one record per issue with a photograph, room, description and required action. Assign a named owner and priority based on whether the issue prevents intended use. A decorative adjustment and an unusable bed should not be treated as the same completion category.'),
 ('Reinspect before closing','Require evidence that the action has actually been completed. Revisit related functions after a repair or replacement. Only then update the care inventory, final room photographs and handover documents. Keep open items visible to the person deciding readiness.')],
 'A hypothetical installation arrives with the correct dining table and chairs, but a drawer unit prevents a bedroom door from opening fully. The punch list identifies a layout problem, assigns the designer to revise placement and requires an updated room photo before closure.',
 ['Room and issue identifier','Approved item or plan reference','Observed problem and photo','Effect on intended guest use','Action owner and target date','Required repair or replacement','Completion evidence','Reinspection result and close date']),
('vacation-rental-spare-parts-inventory','Build a useful spare-parts inventory for rental furniture',
 'Spare inventory should respond to real replacement needs and storage capacity. Start with items that are vulnerable, frequently used or difficult to replace quickly. A large box of unidentified hardware is not a maintenance system.', [
 ('Choose candidates deliberately','List furniture and fixtures with replaceable components. Ask the supplier for exact model compatibility and ordering details. Prioritize a small number of parts that your local team can install appropriately; specialist repairs need a different response plan.'),
 ('Label every spare','Use the property, room, model and component identifier. Keep installation instructions and original fastener details with the record. Avoid mixing hardware that looks similar but has different dimensions or load requirements. Record what is on site and what must be ordered.'),
 ('Control storage and access','Keep textiles dry, protect finishes and use storage conditions appropriate to the product. Define who may retrieve and install parts. Ensure owner storage remains separate from guest supplies and that the local team can find the relevant record when the owner is away.'),
 ('Reorder after use','Record every withdrawal and the issue it resolved. Establish a sensible minimum quantity from actual usage and lead times. Review obsolete inventory when furniture models change so the team does not rely on parts that no longer match the property.')],
 'A hypothetical host stores a spare sofa cover, labeled for the exact model, along with care instructions and a reorder link. When the cover is used, the cleaner logs the change and the owner checks whether to replenish it. A cover from the previous sofa is removed from active inventory.',
 ['Property, room and furniture model','Component and compatible revision','Supplier and ordering reference','Quantity on hand and minimum','Storage location','Authorized installer or specialist','Last use and reason','Reorder status and obsolete-stock review']),
('rental-furnishing-quote-normalization','Compare rental furnishing quotes using the same scope',
 'Two proposals with the same headline label can assign very different responsibilities. Normalize the scope before comparing totals, and separate confirmed inclusions from assumptions that still require an answer.', [
 ('Start with one room and inventory brief','Give both providers the same property details, guest plan, rooms, retained furniture and target date. Specify whether the project includes outdoor areas, kitchen inventory, textiles and photography preparation. A provider cannot price a complete brief that changes between conversations.'),
 ('Separate goods from services','List design, purchasing, receiving, inspection, storage, assembly, installation and packaging removal independently. Record whether each is included, excluded or undecided. Request the basis for allowances and any markups or handling charges rather than treating an estimate as a fixed total.'),
 ('Compare change and delay terms','Ask how substitutions, damaged goods, extra site visits and unavailable inventory are handled. Confirm approval responsibilities and payment milestones. A low design fee may leave the owner with coordination work that another proposal includes.'),
 ('Make the remaining gaps explicit','Write down every unresolved item before selecting a proposal. Put the final responsibilities into the agreement and shared tracker. Use your time, local team capacity and desired control to interpret the comparison; price alone does not capture those differences.')],
 'A hypothetical owner compares a design-only quote with a quote that includes installation. Adding receiving, assembly and local coordination to the first proposal changes the comparison. The owner can still prefer it, but the choice now reflects the same intended result.',
 ['Room and inventory scope','Goods allowance and assumptions','Design and procurement fees','Receiving, storage and freight','Assembly, install and removal','Owner responsibilities','Changes, delays and exclusions','Confirmed total and unresolved items']),
('rental-room-photo-readiness-checklist','A photo-readiness checklist after furnishing a rental',
 'A room is ready for photography when the visible setup matches what guests will find. Use a readiness check to coordinate the installer, cleaner, owner and photographer before everyone arrives on site.', [
 ('Confirm the finished inventory','Check furniture, lamps, bedding and practical accessories against the approved room list. Remove delivery packaging and temporary substitutes that will not remain for guest use. Record missing items that would change the room’s appearance or function.'),
 ('Test the visible functions','Operate blinds, lights, drawers and convertible furniture. Confirm bulbs and controls work and the staged arrangement preserves circulation. Check mirrors, surfaces and textiles in the actual light rather than relying on a remote installation photo.'),
 ('Prepare a truthful shot list','Show room relationships, storage and important amenities in addition to decorative details. Agree with the photographer which areas are finished and which require a later visit. Do not stage an amenity that is unavailable for the stay or conceal a material layout limitation.'),
 ('Manage the published images','Track the image set by room and date. After a replacement or redesign, identify which images need updating across booking channels and the website. Keep the final selected set accessible to the person maintaining the listings.')],
 'A hypothetical bedroom is styled before the window treatment arrives. The host records it as incomplete and decides with the photographer whether to postpone that room. A finished living room can be photographed without implying that every room is ready.',
 ['Room and planned shot','Final furniture and textiles present','Lighting and controls checked','Surfaces and glass cleaned','Missing items or temporary substitutes','Photographer decision and date','Selected image filenames','Channels updated and next review']),
('rental-design-project-handover','What belongs in a rental furnishing project handover',
 'The handover connects design decisions to everyday operation. A host needs to know what was installed, how to care for it and who resolves unfinished work. Collect those records before the project team disperses.', [
 ('Record the installed property','Capture the actual furniture and finish schedule, including substitutions. Save room layouts, product identifiers and photographs. Keep the latest version clearly marked so a future order is based on the installed product rather than an early concept board.'),
 ('Build a care and replacement file','Collect manufacturer instructions, cleaning limitations and parts information. Give the turnover team the details needed for routine care, and identify work that requires a specialist. Store receipts and warranty terms with the relevant item instead of in an unrelated email archive.'),
 ('Separate complete work from open work','Attach the punch list with owners and target dates. Record outstanding claims, delayed products and any temporary arrangements. The handover should make it obvious what has been accepted and what still requires action; a final styling photo is not a substitute for that record.'),
 ('Transfer practical ownership','Confirm who manages supplier contacts, returns and replacement approvals after the project. Walk the local team through controls, storage and room reset photos. Schedule a review after initial use so operational problems can be documented and resolved.')],
 'A hypothetical remote owner receives a beautiful photo set but no sofa model number. When a cover is damaged, ordering a matching replacement becomes difficult. A complete handover includes the actual model, finish, care instructions and spare-cover reference alongside the photo.',
 ['Final room layouts and dated photos','Installed inventory and substitutions','Care instructions and restrictions','Receipts and warranty references','Supplier and spare-parts contacts','Open punch-list items and claims','Local team walkthrough completed','Post-launch review owner and date']),
]
